* PCB, process creation\nand context switching
** Process Control Block
*** One per process, kernel-only
*** State, PID, PC, registers, priority
*** Stack, heap, address space
*** Open files, children, exit code
*** Allocated by fork() in the parent
*** Freed after waitpid() collects the status
*** Linux: task_struct
** Process creation
*** fork()
**** Full copy of the parent
**** Returns 0 in the child
**** Returns the child PID in the parent
**** Returns -1 on failure (errno)
*** exec()
**** Replaces the binary image
**** Entry point then main()
**** Library family, execve() is the syscall
*** exit()
**** Ends the process
**** Status stored in the PCB
*** waitpid()
**** Blocks until a child ends
**** Retrieves the status
**** Frees the PCB (no more zombie)
*** Launching a program = fork + exec
** Context switch
*** Triggers
**** exit()
**** Blocking wait (I/O)
**** Scheduler decision (time slice)
*** Steps
**** Save state in outgoing PCB
**** Restore state from incoming PCB
**** Reconfigure the MMU
**** Load the new PC
*** Hardware involved
**** Registers, FPU/VFP, coprocessors
**** MMU, TLB, caches
*** Cost: keep switches rare
@endmindmap
